			],
			"Message": "docs(Vine): Standardize section headers with trailing emojis\n\nRefactor the `Architecture.md` documentation in the `Vine` repository to ensure consistent formatting across all major and minor section headers. Add the appropriate descriptive emoji (e.g., 📋, 🏗️, 🔌, 💻) to every section title, mirroring the style established in the title and recent commits.\n\nThis change improves the visual hierarchy and navigability of the documentation when viewed in Markdown renderers and the GitHub web interface, ensuring a uniform appearance across the entire protocol specification document.\n\nThis is a pure formatting update with no changes to the technical content or links.",
			"SHA": "ac0d58ace57b3e35cbb423ad1e187cbb7686043c"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-15T23:36:33Z",
			"File": [
				"⡿⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				6,
				1
			],
			"Message": "docs(Vine): Enhance Architecture.md with emojis and external references\n\nRefactor the Vine architecture documentation to improve visual hierarchy and developer utility. Add descriptive emojis to section headers (Overview, Architecture, Protocol Buffers, etc.) and the title to aid quick navigation. Convert inline references to terminal commands and code blocks to use consistent backtick formatting for technical terms like `gRPC`, `Rust`, and `TypeScript`.\n\nReformat the protocol file lists, message types, and service definitions to use cleaner bullet points and consistent spacing. Crucially, replace relative Markdown links in the 'Related Documentation' section with absolute GitHub URLs pointing to the `Current` branch of the respective component repositories (Mountain, Cocoon, Air, Grove). This ensures the documentation remains accessible when viewed directly on GitHub, resolving broken links for external readers.\n\nAlso clarify the transport security model by explicitly noting 'localhost-only enforced' in the attributes table.",
			"SHA": "5cde4c554ea81333add96b7f290c15f036bcb4da"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-15T22:32:14Z",
			"File": [
				"⡿⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				6,
				1
			],
			"Message": "docs(Vine): Add Mermaid diagram illustrating gRPC protocol layer structure\n\nIntroduce a comprehensive Mermaid graph in the Vine architecture documentation to visualize the relationships between Protocol Buffer definitions (Vine, Spine, Grove, Air) and their generated Rust/TypeScript implementations.\n\nThe diagram clarifies:\n- The distinct service contracts: Vine.proto (ExtensionHost lifecycle/commands), Spine.proto (action/response pattern), Grove.proto (WASM host), and Air.proto (background services).\n- The code generation workflow using `prost-build` for Rust and `protoc-gen-ts` for TypeScript targets.\n- The communication topology showing Mountain as the central server and Cocoon, Air, and Grove as gRPC clients.\n\nThis visual aid complements the existing textual overview, providing developers with an immediate reference for the IPC contract structure and component interactions within the Land ecosystem.",
			"SHA": "2c1f8207d1935d98f378b502f055db3ce8723c44"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-15T22:24:30Z",
			"File": [
				"⡿⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				6,
				1
			],
			"Message": "feat(Vine): Add comprehensive gRPC protocol layer architecture documentation\n\nIntroduce the initial architecture documentation for the Vine protocol layer within the documentation submodule. This new file defines the gRPC service contracts, message types, and communication patterns used for inter-component IPC between Mountain, Cocoon, Air, and Grove.\n\nThe document details:\n- Protocol Buffer definitions for `ExtensionHost`, `Spine`, and supporting messages (Init, Command, Language Features).\n- Service directionality and triggers for lifecycle management, extension activation, and editor feature requests (hover, completions, etc.).\n- Client (Cocoon/TypeScript) and Server (Mountain/Rust) implementation patterns using `@grpc/grpc-js` and `tonic`.\n- Port allocation strategy (defaulting to 50051 for main services) and code generation workflows for both Rust and TypeScript targets.\n\nThis provides the primary reference for developers implementing or consuming the strongly-typed gRPC contract in the Land ecosystem.",
			"SHA": "2c8d0a2e856a12c7311bb477e390c2326503303a"
		}
